PRIVATE REFLECTION: {
The deliberation remains divided between A (Abduction) and F (Extension). 

The clinical prompt directly couples physician-applied resistance to an extended right leg while evaluating a confirmatory action in the left lateral decubitus position. Kinesiologically, an action performed against resistance indicates active voluntary muscle contraction; active resisted hip extension unloads the iliopsoas via reciprocal inhibition, whereas the Cope psoas test is strictly passive. The maneuver as written directly describes the resisted hip abduction test (Lequesne test) for the gluteal abductor complex, which is innervated by the superior gluteal nerve (L4–S1). Choice A (Abduction) remains the only fully consistent and defensible answer.
}

